  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: aef08fe7bbe81412eaacd58c23b63316, type: 3}
  m_Name: silver_spellbook
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::Data.ScriptableObjects.StoryBookDefinition
  Id: silver_spellbook
  Title: The Silver Spellbook
  Author: Hazel Thistlewick
  Color: {r: 0.78, g: 0.82, b: 0.9, a: 1}
  Hint: Somewhere in the forest clearing, among the stumps
  Pages:
  - 'In the village of Thimble Cross there lived a witch named Marigold who could
    not remember a single spell.


    She wrote them on the backs of her hands,
    on the cuffs of her sleeves, and once, in a moment of real desperation, on Pudding,
    her very patient cat. Pudding walked about for a week with a cure for hiccups
    across his tummy, and never quite forgave her.'
  - 'One winter morning Marigold went into the forest for kindling and found a ring
    of old stumps, white with snow. In the middle of the ring lay a book with a silver
    cover.


    Its pages were empty. But when she held it to her ear, it hummed,
    the way a kettle hums just before it sings.


    She tucked it under her shawl
    and carried it home.'
  - 'That night, as Marigold fumbled for the words to light a candle, something glimmered
    on the empty page. Letters were appearing, one at a time, written in moonlight.


    The
    book was remembering the spell for her.


    It waited patiently while she sounded
    the words out. The candle lit. Pudding, who had been bracing himself to be written
    on, let out a long breath.'
  - 'After that, Marigold became rather good at magic.


    She mended Mrs Hobb''s
    teacups, coaxed the sulkiest kettle in the village to boil, and charmed the baker''s
    oven so the bread came out golden every time. People began to knock on her door.


    They
    knocked when something was broken, and they thanked her very kindly, and then
    they went home.'
  - 'And every evening Marigold sat at her table with one bowl, one spoon and one
    cat, and the cottage felt very large.


    So one night she opened the silver
    book and asked it for a spell to make friends.


    The page stayed empty.


    She
    asked the next night, and the night after that. Nothing. Not one silvery letter.'
  - '"Useless thing," she said at last, though she didn''t really mean it, and she
    shut the book away in the dresser drawer.


    That was the night the great
    storm came. Snow piled against the doors, the wind howled down the chimneys,
    and a little before midnight the mill-keeper''s roof gave way under the weight
    of it all.'
  - 'Marigold heard the shouting and ran out into the snow. She was halfway down
    the lane before she realised the silver book was still in the drawer at home.


    She
    tried to remember a warming spell. A mending spell. Any spell at all. Her head
    was as empty as the book''s pages had been.


    So she did the only thing she
    could think of. She shouted, "Everyone to mine!"'
  - 'She lit her fire with ordinary matches. She made soup in her biggest pot, and
    when that ran out, in her second biggest. She found blankets for the mill-keeper''s
    children and a cushion for old Mr Penny''s knees.


    There was singing. There
    was a game of cards that got very serious. Pudding was passed from lap to lap
    and pretended to hate every minute of it.'
  - 'In the grey morning, with everyone asleep in heaps around the hearth, Marigold
    crept to the dresser and opened the drawer.


    The silver book had written
    a single line, in letters that glowed like embers:


    "There. You knew that
    one all along."'
  - 'Then, one by one, its pages filled with names. Mrs Hobb. The mill-keeper and
    his four children. Old Mr Penny. The baker, and the baker''s terrible dog.


    Marigold
    never did learn to remember a spell without help.


    But she never once forgot
    a birthday.'
